    "id": "C08",
    "title": "File:Princetown (AU), Port Campbell National Park, Twelve Apostles -- 2019 -- 0969.jpg",
    "file_page_url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3APrincetown_%28AU%29%2C_Port_Campbell_National_Park%2C_Twelve_Apostles_--_2019_--_0969.jpg",
    "original_url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d7/Princetown_%28AU%29%2C_Port_Campbell_National_Park%2C_Twelve_Apostles_--_2019_--_0969.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
    "thumbnail_url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Princetown_%28AU%29%2C_Port_Campbell_National_Park%2C_Twelve_Apostles_--_2019_--_0969.jpg/960px-Princetown_%28AU%29%2C_Port_Campbell_National_Park%2C_Twelve_Apostles_--_2019_--_0969.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "local_path": "references/candidates/08_princetown_au_port_campbell_national_park_twelve_apostles_2019_0969_jpg.jpg",
    "width": 6704,
    "height": 4469,
    "mime": "image/jpeg",
    "author": "<bdi><a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q34788025\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q34788025\"><span title=\"German photographer and mathematician\">Dietmar Rabich</span></a></bdi>",
    "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
    "description": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Twelve_Apostles_(Victoria)\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:The Twelve Apostles (Victoria)\">Twelve Apostles</a> at <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_Campbell_National_Park\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Port Campbell National Park\">Port Campbell National Park</a>, Princetown, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Victoria_(Australia)\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Victoria (Australia)\">Victoria</a>, Australia<br><small>Port Campbell <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_park\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:National park\">National Park</a> is located on the south coast of Victoria along the Great Ocean Road and protects a spectacular coastal landscape of limestone, cliffs, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cave\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Cave\">caves</a>, and natural bridges.<br>Its geological formation dates back millions of years: over thousands of years, layers of limestone and shells formed caves, gateways, and towers, which were repeatedly eroded by the Southern Ocean. The coast also served as a habitat for indigenous groups in the region, including the Wergaia and Eastern Maar communities. From the 1950s/60s onwards, the area was designated a protected area to preserve its exceptional geology, wildlife, and flora and to manage visitor flows in a sensible manner.<br>Special features include the famous limestone cliffs, narrow passages, natural bridges, and steep cliffs; highlights such as the Twelve Apostles, Loch Ard Gorge, and other coastal formations attract many visitors each year. The sea is home to seals, seabirds, and rich marine fauna, while the rocky shores also support maritime flora. Hiking trails, lookout points, and boat tours offer insights into geology, geomorphology, and coastal protection—a harmonious combination of natural wonders and historical perspective.</small>",
    "downloaded_at": "2026-08-18T09:48:02Z"
  },
  {
    "id": "C09",
    "title": "File:Blue Mountains National Park (AU), Three Sisters -- 2019 -- 1987-9.jpg",
    "file_page_url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_Mountains_National_Park_%28AU%29%2C_Three_Sisters_--_2019_--_1987-9.jpg",
    "original_url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/de/Blue_Mountains_National_Park_%28AU%29%2C_Three_Sisters_--_2019_--_1987-9.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
    "thumbnail_url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Blue_Mountains_National_Park_%28AU%29%2C_Three_Sisters_--_2019_--_1987-9.jpg/960px-Blue_Mountains_National_Park_%28AU%29%2C_Three_Sisters_--_2019_--_1987-9.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "local_path": "references/candidates/09_blue_mountains_national_park_au_three_sisters_2019_1987_9_jpg.jpg",
    "width": 5973,
    "height": 6191,
    "mime": "image/jpeg",
    "author": "<bdi><a href=\"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q34788025\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"d:Q34788025\"><span title=\"German photographer and mathematician\">Dietmar Rabich</span></a></bdi>",
    "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
    "description": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Three_Sisters_(Australia)\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Three Sisters (Australia)\">Three Sisters</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Mountains_National_Park\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Blue Mountains National Park\">Blue Mountains National Park</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_South_Wales\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:New South Wales\">New South Wales</a>, Australia<br><small>The Blue Mountains <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_park\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:National park\">National Park</a> is located west of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sydney\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Sydney\">Sydney</a> and is one of Australia’s most famous natural areas. Its history is closely linked to Aboriginal culture, particularly that of the Gundungurra and Darug peoples, who have inhabited the area for tens of thousands of years and have a spiritual connection to the landscape.<br>For European settlers, the rugged sandstone plateaus were long considered an insurmountable obstacle. It was not until 1813 that Gregory Blaxland, William Lawson, and William Charles Wentworth succeeded in crossing the mountains, opening the way for the development of the Australian hinterland.<br>In the 19th century, parts of the region were used for logging and mining, while at the same time its scenic appeal was discovered. In 1959, the Blue Mountains National Park was officially established to protect the unique eucalyptus vegetation, deep gorges, and waterfalls.<br>In 2000, UNESCO declared the area part of the Greater Blue Mountains Area a World Heritage Site. Today, the park stands for nature conservation, tourism, and recognition of Australia’s indigenous cultural history.</small>",
    "downloaded_at": "2026-08-18T09:48:31Z"
  },
